==English==

===Etymology===
From {{m|en|times}}.

===Proper noun===
{{en-prop}}

# {{lb|en|newspapers}} A common name (often in combination) for a [[newspaper]] or [[periodical]], especially ''{{w|The Times}}'' (published in the [[United Kingdom]]), but also ''{{w|The New York Times}}'', ''{{w|The Times of India}}'', ''{{w|Radio Times}}''<!--magazine-->, etc.
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|page=73|passage=Mr. Campion seated himself in the client’s chair and crossed his long legs. His hat, his gloves and his folded '''''Times''''' newspaper he held upon his knee.}}
#* {{RQ:Slate|author=Max Read|title=What Is “The Current Thing”?|url=https://slate.com/technology/2022/05/current-thing-meme-twitter-elon-musk-marc-andreessen.html|date=2022-05-09|passage=From there, the meme will slowly leak into more popular mainstream-right punditry, and then into the Republican Party, until Ted Cruz sweatily asks a judicial nominee if they “support the current thing” and the '''Times''' writes some stilted, breathless article about the meme as disinformation.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260524193706/https://slate.com/technology/2022/05/current-thing-meme-twitter-elon-musk-marc-andreessen.html|archivedate=24 May 2026}}
# {{surname|en|}}.

